================================================================================
METHOD — STANDING-ORDERS §7 (scapy, not ping)
================================================================================
A ping cannot read a lease time. Every leg sends a crafted DHCPDISCOVER with a unique chaddr
and an explicit option 55 parameter request list that ASKS for option 51 (and 58/59), then
reads option 51 out of the captured OFFER and the captured ACK. The verdict rests on those
frames; `show ip dhcp pool` and `show ip dhcp binding` are corroboration only, read after the
wire, never instead of it.

================================================================================
STEP 1 — a 120-day lease is delivered as option 51 = 10,368,000 in OFFER and ACK
================================================================================
  sudo -n python3.8 dhcp4.py client eth3 --mac 02:c4:00:00:01:01 --prl 1,3,6,51,58,59 \
      --arp-watch 192.168.10.0/24 --wait 20 --pcap leg1-120d.pcap --json leg1-120d.json

  Every DHCP frame of the transaction, as decoded from the capture:
      DISCOVER  yiaddr=0.0.0.0          opt51=None       opt58=None  opt59=None
      OFFER     yiaddr=192.168.10.100   opt51=10368000   opt58=None  opt59=None
      REQUEST   yiaddr=0.0.0.0          opt51=None       opt58=None  opt59=None
      ACK       yiaddr=192.168.10.100   opt51=10368000   opt58=None  opt59=None

  opt51 = 10,368,000 s = 120 d 0 h 0 m 0 s, in BOTH the OFFER and the ACK. STEP 1 PASS.

  Options 58 (renewal/T1) and 59 (rebinding/T2) are ABSENT even though the client explicitly
  requested them in option 55. Recorded, not required — ISC dhcpd does not emit T1/T2 unless
  configured, so requiring them would be a false FAIL. (Independently confirmed in T43851
  step 1, where a 1-day lease likewise carried neither.)

  Corroboration, `show ip dhcp binding` (work/binding-120d.log), read AFTER the frames:
      show clock      -> UTC Time: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 02:33:57 +0000
      IP Address      ClientId          Type    Expiry
      192.168.10.100  02c4.0000.0101    Dynamic 12 Feb 2027 02:33:39
  Granted 15 Oct 2026 02:33:39 UTC, expiring 12 Feb 2027 02:33:39 UTC — 16+30+31+31+12 = exactly
  120 days, to the second. This evidence is only readable because the DUT's year-2000 RTC was
  repaired with `clock set` at group setup (see T43851's log).

================================================================================
STEP 2 — THE BOUNDARY: 120 accepted, 121 refused. This IS the 30->120 day feature.
================================================================================
Sent through ckcon.py (NOT the stop-on-error driver) so the rejections are captured rather
than aborting the batch — the refusals are the expected result here, work/boundary.log:

  awplus(dhcp-config)# lease 121 0 0
                                  ^
  % Invalid input detected at '^' marker.

  awplus(dhcp-config)# lease 120 0 0
  awplus(dhcp-config)#                     <- accepted silently, no "% " line

  awplus(dhcp-config)# lease 999 0 0
                                  ^
  % Invalid input detected at '^' marker.

The parser accepts exactly up to 120 days and refuses 121 and 999 — the documented range
(`lease.md`, "<days> ... from 0 to 120") holds on sin_x96056-20261012-24, and the old 30-day
IP Infusion ceiling is gone. STEP 2 PASS.

================================================================================
STEP 3 — THE PAIR: other lease values must yield their own option 51, same run
================================================================================
Three successive IN-PLACE changes (no `no lease` between them, so each overwrites a live
value), each followed by a fresh client and graded on the captured OFFER/ACK:

  | configured      | expected opt51 | measured OFFER | measured ACK | chaddr            |
  | --------------- | -------------- | -------------- | ------------ | ----------------- |
  | lease 1 0 0     |         86,400 |         86,400 |       86,400 | 02:c4:00:00:01:02 |
  | lease 0 1 0     |          3,600 |          3,600 |        3,600 | 02:c4:00:00:01:03 |
  | lease 120 0 0   |     10,368,000 |     10,368,000 |   10,368,000 | 02:c4:00:00:01:04 |

Every value matched exactly, in both messages. The 1-day row is the control the 120-day claim
rests on: identical crafted DISCOVER, identical pool, only the configured lease differs, and
option 51 moves with it. The 1-hour row adds a third point so the result is a curve rather
than a two-point coincidence, and proves sub-day granularity.

This also answers a question raised from the console feed mid-run (silent-discard concern, the
shape of this platform's mirroring defect M2, `platforms/x960.md` §5): every change above was an
in-place overwrite of a LIVE value and every one took effect on the wire immediately. There is
no M2-shaped silent discard for `lease`, and `show ip dhcp pool` agreed with the wire at every
point. The pool read of `<1:0:0:0>` that prompted the question was taken after STEP 4's
`no lease`, where 1 day is the correct default.

================================================================================
STEP 4 — REMOVAL: `no lease` restores the 1-day default and clears the config
================================================================================
  awplus(dhcp-config)# no lease            (cfg.py exit 0, no "% " lines)
  show ip dhcp pool -> lease <days:hours:minutes:seconds> <1:0:0:0>     (the documented default)

  Proven on the wire, not just in the show — fresh client 02:c4:00:00:01:05:
      OFFER 192.168.10.104   opt51 = 86400
      ACK                    opt51 = 86400, renewal None, rebinding None

  show running-config, pool stanza after removal — the lease line is GONE:
      ip dhcp pool CKPOOL
       network 192.168.10.0 255.255.255.0
       range 192.168.10.100 192.168.10.110
      !
STEP 4 PASS.

